--- Threads on hosted x86-64 Linux (V2, stage 57): `spawn` is one `clone`
--- system call with a stack the caller owns, `join` waits on the word the
--- kernel clears when the thread ends. Nothing is allocated here: the
--- stack and the thread's word are the caller's memory, as the scheduler's
--- task table is a kernel's. A thread is its id word, a `u32` the caller
--- keeps (one element of a view: `tids[i..i + 1]`), stored by the kernel
--- when the thread starts (CLONE_PARENT_SETTID) and cleared, with a futex
--- wake, when it ends (CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID). A thread's entry is a
--- procedure with no parameters and no result; its address comes from a
--- machine block (`mov rax, worker; out rax -> e`), as for
--- `core.x64.sched`. The system call is written as a `machine x64` block,
--- so this module is x86-64 only for now: a program that imports it does
--- not compile for AArch64 (E0900 at the block). `thread` is a reserved
--- word, hence `std.threads`.
module std.threads
import std.os

--- CLONE_VM, FS, FILES, SIGHAND, THREAD, SYSVSEM, PARENT_SETTID and
--- CHILD_CLEARTID: a thread of this process, sharing everything, its id
--- written into the word and cleared from it.
CLONE_FLAGS : u64 := 0x350F00
FUTEX : word := 202
FUTEX_WAIT : u64 := 0

--- Starts a thread at `start_at` on `tstack` (256 bytes at least, its top
--- aligned down to 16) and records it in `slot[0]`: the thread's id, or
--- the kernel's -errno (-22 for a stack too short or an empty slot). The
--- child begins on its own stack with the entry address as the only word
--- on it: it pops that, pushes the address of the exit stub and the entry,
--- and `ret`s into the entry with rsp at 8 mod 16, as after a call; when
--- the entry returns it lands in the stub, which ends this thread alone
--- (`exit`, 60). The parent leaves the block with the kernel's answer in
--- rax. The fifth argument, the TLS in r8, is zero: no CLONE_SETTLS.
pub proc spawn(slot : rw view u32, tstack : rw view u8, start_at : u64) -> word
    permit cpu.asm, memory.raw
    if tstack.len < 256 then ret -22
    if slot.len < 1 then ret -22
    top := (u64(tstack.addr) + u64(tstack.len)) & ~u64(15)
    [addr u64 (top - 8)] <- start_at
    child := top - 8
    ta := u64(slot.addr)
    zero : u64 <- 0
    r : word <- 0
    machine x64
        in rdi <- CLONE_FLAGS
        in rsi <- child
        in rdx <- ta
        in r10 <- ta
        in r8 <- zero
        mov eax, 56
        syscall
        test rax, rax
        jnz .parent
        pop rax
        lea rcx, [.done]
        push rcx
        push rax
        ret
    .done:
        mov eax, 60
        xor edi, edi
        syscall
    .parent:
        out rax -> r
        clobber rcx, r11
    end
    ret r
end

--- Waits until the thread whose word is `slot[0]` has ended: the word is
--- read, then `futex` WAIT on it while it still holds that id — the kernel
--- wakes the word when it clears it. Returns at once for a thread that has
--- ended, one never started, or an empty slot.
pub proc join(slot : view u32)
    permit os.syscall
    if slot.len < 1 then ret
    loop
        v := slot[0]
        if v == 0 then ret
        os.syscall(FUTEX, u64(slot.addr), FUTEX_WAIT, u64(v), 0)
    end
end
